# Pi-Northstar environment variables
# Copy to .env or set in your shell profile (~/.zshrc, ~/.bashrc)
# Process environment wins over .env

# ── API keys (optional — zero-config DuckDuckGo works without any) ──

# GITHUB_TOKEN="ghp_..."
# EXA_API_KEY="..."
# BRAVE_API_KEY="..."
# TAVILY_API_KEY="..."
# YOUTUBE_API_KEY="..."
# REDDIT_CLIENT_ID="..."
# REDDIT_CLIENT_SECRET="..."
# REDDIT_USER_AGENT="pi-northstar/0.1"
# SEARXNG_BASE_URL="https://searxng.example.com"
# NITTER_BASE_URL="https://nitter.example.com"
# LISTENNOTES_API_KEY="..."
# PRODUCTHUNT_API_TOKEN="..."
# PATENTSVIEW_API_KEY="..."

# ── Research source keys (optional; Pi conventions, not vendor-standard names) ──
# All research sources work unauthenticated; these keys only raise quota limits.
# SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_API_KEY="..."
# OPENALEX_API_KEY="..."
# NCBI_API_KEY="..."        # PubMed E-utilities optional key
# NCBI_EMAIL="you@example.com"
# STACKEXCHANGE_KEY="..."

# ── Diffbot (optional, paid — read README privacy warning first) ──
# Setting DIFFBOT_TOKEN sends queries, URLs, selectors, and text to Diffbot endpoints.
# Operator limits are defaults/caps: resolveDiffbotSpend validates once per kg call
# and rejects out-of-range before any paid call (never clamps).
# No behavior changes without DIFFBOT_TOKEN.
# DIFFBOT_TOKEN="..."
# DIFFBOT_SEARCH_SIZE="10"        # default 10, max 50 per provider
# DIFFBOT_ENHANCE_SIZE="1"        # default 1, max 10 per provider
# DIFFBOT_NLP_MAX_CHARS="100000"  # hard cap 100000
# DIFFBOT_MAX_PROVIDERS="3"       # default 3, max 8
# DIFFBOT_FALLBACK_BUDGET="3"     # default 3, max 25 per fetch, 0 disables

# ── SPARQL graph (optional, operator-owned endpoint — no Diffbot needed) ──
# Setting GRAPH_SPARQL_ENDPOINT registers the graph tool with language 'sparql'
# (SELECT/ASK only; SERVICE, dataset (FROM), and updates reject before dispatch). Endpoint is
# env-only, never model input: http/https, no embedded credentials; blank =
# unconfigured. The token travels via Authorization header only and is
# redacted from errors and status output.
# GRAPH_SPARQL_ENDPOINT="https://sparql.example.com/query"
# GRAPH_SPARQL_TOKEN="..."

# ── Firecrawl + Jina (optional, paid/external — read README privacy warning first) ──
# Setting these keys sends queries/URLs to fixed vendor hosts only, plus
# vendor-side external processing of page reads. No cookies, browser state,
# or caller headers are forwarded. Selection/AI controls below stay env-only.
# FIRECRAWL_API_KEY="..."
# JINA_API_KEY="..."

# ── Extended web search providers (optional, paid/external — read README privacy warning first) ──
# Each key enables its backend for explicit PI_SEARCH_WEB_BACKENDS lists only;
# none join the automatic top-3 preference order. Selection stays env-only.
# Credential requirements vary by provider: most need only their API key, but
# Bright Data needs BOTH BRIGHTDATA_API_KEY and BRIGHTDATA_SERP_ZONE.
# PARALLEL_API_KEY="..."
# TINYFISH_API_KEY="..."
# QUERIT_API_KEY="..."
# VALYU_API_KEY="..."
# BOCHA_API_KEY="..."
# XCRAWL_API_KEY="..."
# XAI_API_KEY="..."
# MISTRAL_API_KEY="..."
# BRIGHTDATA_API_KEY="..."
# BRIGHTDATA_SERP_ZONE="..."  # required together with BRIGHTDATA_API_KEY
# SERPAPI_KEY="..."
# SERPER_API_KEY="..."

# ── Native Pi tool exposure (operator-owned, env-only; default-deny) ──
# Native Northstar tools are disabled by default, regardless of configured credentials.
# Unset or blank means zero native tools and an inert backend; CLI still works.
# Enable exact canonical names only with comma-separated PI_SEARCH_NATIVE_TOOLS
# (owner: PUBLIC_TOOL_ALLOWLIST_ENV_VAR in src/capabilities.ts). Valid names —
# exactly these 9, verbatim (PUBLIC_TOOL_NAMES):
#   web_search, fetch, github, social, kg, graph, browser, desktop, agent
# Unknown, legacy (e.g. `video`), internal (e.g. `media`), duplicate, or malformed
# names reject at startup (parsePublicToolAllowlist). No spaces around commas.
# Examples:
# PI_SEARCH_NATIVE_TOOLS=""   # default-deny: zero tools
# PI_SEARCH_NATIVE_TOOLS="web_search"   # search-only
# PI_SEARCH_NATIVE_TOOLS="web_search,fetch,github,social,kg,graph,browser,desktop,agent"   # full local surface
# ── Web search selection + native AI + fetch routing (env-only, no JSON mapping) ──
# Absent/blank PI_SEARCH_WEB_BACKENDS selects the first 3 configured backends in
# preference order (tavily, exa, brave, diffbot, firecrawl, jina, searxng,
# ollama-search, duckduckgo; codex is explicit-only). Explicit lists run all
# runnable entries concurrently (max 8; duplicates/unknown/>8 reject before calls).
# Extended backends below are explicit-list only and never automatic.
# PI_SEARCH_WEB_BACKENDS="tavily,exa,brave"
# Extended aliases: parallel, parallel-mcp, tinyfish, querit, valyu, bocha,
# xcrawl, xai, mistral, brightdata, serpapi, serper
# e.g. PI_SEARCH_WEB_BACKENDS="tavily,exa,serper,xcrawl"
# PI_SEARCH_WEB_PROVIDER_TIMEOUT_MS="12000"   # integer 1000..30000
# TAVILY_RESEARCH_MODEL="pro"               # mini|pro|auto; legacy/internal direct native report adapter only (registered Pi agent mode uses adaptive jobs)
# PI_SEARCH_NATIVE_SUMMARIES="1"              # 1/true/0/false, default on
# PI_SEARCH_NATIVE_ANSWERS="1"                # 1/true/0/false, default on
# PI_SEARCH_KG_ENRICHMENT="0"                 # 1/true/0/false, default off
# PI_SEARCH_EXTERNAL_FETCH="0"                # 1/true/0/false, default off
# PI_SEARCH_FETCH_BACKENDS="firecrawl,jina"   # ordered unique subset, max 2
# PI_SEARCH_FETCH_PROVIDER_TIMEOUT_MS="15000" # integer 1000..30000

# ── Codex/ChatGPT web search (optional, auto-detected) ──
# By default Pi-Northstar reads ${CODEX_HOME:-~/.codex}/auth.json from `codex login`.
# Environment credentials override that file.
# CODEX_ACCESS_TOKEN="..."
# CODEX_ACCOUNT_ID="..."
# CODEX_HOME="$HOME/.codex"

# ── Embedding sidecar (powers GitHub code_search semantic retrieval) ──
# When configured, github code_search uses AST-aware embeddings + tree-sitter
# for semantic code retrieval. Without these, code_search falls back to
# lexical GitHub code search.

# EMBEDDING_SIDECAR_PROVIDER="openai"
# EMBEDDING_SIDECAR_BASE_URL="https://api.openai.com/v1"
# EMBEDDING_SIDECAR_API_TOKEN="sk-..."
# EMBEDDING_SIDECAR_DIMENSIONS="1536"
# EMBEDDING_SIDECAR_CODE_MODEL="text-embedding-3-small"

# ── LLM (used by deep research and other LLM-backed features) ──

# SEARCH_LLM_PROVIDER="openai"
# SEARCH_LLM_API_TOKEN="sk-..."
# SEARCH_LLM_BASE_URL="https://api.openai.com/v1"

# ── Deep research ──

# DEEP_RESEARCH_BASE_URL="https://..."
# DEEP_RESEARCH_WORKER_BASE_URL="https://..."
# DEEP_RESEARCH_API_TOKEN="..."
# DEEP_RESEARCH_MODEL="gpt-4o"
# DEEP_RESEARCH_WORKER_MODEL="gpt-4o-mini"

# ── Crawl4AI (alternative page reader backend) ──

# CRAWL4AI_BASE_URL="https://..."
# CRAWL4AI_API_TOKEN="..."

# ── Ollama search (local LLM-backed search) ──

# OLLAMA_SEARCH_BASE_URL="http://localhost:11434"
# OLLAMA_SEARCH_API_KEY="..."

# ── Leaf-runtime RPC (pi-subagents co-installed extension, optional) ──
# Legacy exact leaf-model fallback. Prefer `/northstar model provider/model`
# (or PI_NORTHSTAR_MODEL) plus `/northstar agent on|off` for new setups.
# PI_NORTHSTAR_LEAF_MODEL="anthropic/claude-opus-4-5"
# Exact 0 is the hard off override even when file config says agent on.
# PI_NORTHSTAR_AGENT_STEERING="0"
# ── Unified operator model (local leaf runtime + adaptive steering; fetch answer uses session model only) ──
# Exact provider/model id, operator-owned. Precedence: PI_NORTHSTAR_MODEL env
# > ~/.pi-northstar/config.json modelId > PI_NORTHSTAR_LEAF_MODEL. The unified
# id is now agent-only: fetch answer reuses the session model and rejects any
# per-call answerModel value; it fails closed when no session model exists
# (never invents a model). Auth always
# comes from Pi ModelRuntime auth.json; .env keys never fund answer calls.
# PI_NORTHSTAR_MODEL="anthropic/claude-opus-4-5"
# Optional answer-mode context window (tokens) for page-extract budgeting
# (60% of context minus a 4k-char safety margin; over-budget extracts truncate
# with notice, full text stays in the responseId store). Positive integer.
# PI_NORTHSTAR_MODEL_CONTEXT_TOKENS="128000"

# ── Vision / multimodal (optional; see README before enabling remote routes) ──
# Destination configuration and feature activation are separate gates.
# Current fetch integrations are explicit opt-in:
# PI_VISION_FETCH_DESCRIBE="1"       # direct image URL: add generated description
# PI_VISION_FETCH_VIDEO_FRAMES="1"  # YouTube fetch: anonymous yt-dlp/ffmpeg keyframes
#
# Normal PDF fetch stays local through unpdf. Sparse/scanned pages warn.
# PDF tier bounds are code-owned (src/web/access/web-access-pdf.ts), not env:
# 20 MiB / 100 pages / 50k chars / 30s wall clock; answer mode additionally
# surfaces per-page markdown under details.pdf.markdown.
# This flag is accepted but fail-closed until a PDF page-image renderer ships:
# PI_VISION_PDF_CLOUD_RENDER="1"
# Local operator video files (fetch URL names an .mp4/.mov/.webm/… file):
# interrogated with fixed-argv ffmpeg/ffprobe under the same tier-gated
# vision path as YouTube (no Files upload, no new credentials). Size ceiling
# is operator-lowerable only via this variable (MiB, positive int ≤ 50):
# PI_VISION_VIDEO_MAX_SIZE_MB="50"
# Full-file Gemini video fallback (default off; exact value required). Behind
# PI_VISION_VIDEO_GEMINI="1" only, and only when local keyframes yield
# nothing: Files API resumable upload + prompt + file URI query with the
# existing Developer key (needs PI_VISION_GEMINI_ENABLED="1" + GEMINI_API_KEY;
# Vertex/ADC has no REST path and warns), with DELETE cleanup. Gemini Web is
# available only with this flag + PI_VISION_GEMINI_WEB_ENABLED="1". A live
# /chrome-authorize session is preferred; because the companion authority does
# not expose local-file upload, full-file attachment can fall back to a fresh
# isolated browser seeded from the sensitive Google session explicitly imported
# by /reach-setup. Without a usable session it degrades closed. Default off:
# zero video bytes leave the box.
# PI_VISION_VIDEO_GEMINI="1"
#
# OpenAI-compatible vision endpoint (loopback or cloud) + exact model IDs.
# Loopback can be keyless; a remote endpoint sends admitted image/keyframe bytes
# to that operator-selected service when a feature gate above is enabled.
# PI_VISION_OPENAI_COMPAT_BASE_URL="http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1"
# PI_VISION_OPENAI_COMPAT_MODEL="llava"   # comma-separated exact model IDs, no normalization
# PI_VISION_OPENAI_COMPAT_API_KEY="..."   # optional; omit for keyless loopback endpoints
#
# Gemini Developer API or Vertex. Exact enable value is required.
# PI_VISION_GEMINI_ENABLED="1"
# PI_VISION_GEMINI_MODEL="gemini-2.0-flash"  # exact model; this is also the current default
# GEMINI_API_KEY="..."                       # Developer API
# GOOGLE_GENAI_API_KEY="..."                 # alternative Developer API key name
# For Vertex, use ADC plus all of the following:
# GOOGLE_GENAI_USE_VERTEXAI="1"
# GOOGLE_VERTEX_PROJECT="my-project"
# GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT="my-project"           # alternative project env name
# GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION="us-central1"
#
# Gemini Web is a separate disabled-default seam behind both exact opt-ins:
# PI_VISION_GEMINI_WEB_ENABLED="1" + PI_VISION_VIDEO_GEMINI="1". A live
# /chrome-authorize lease is preferred when it can satisfy the requested Web
# operation. Full-file video also needs a code-owned file attachment primitive;
# if the live companion lease cannot provide one, Northstar can use a fresh
# isolated agent-browser session seeded from the Google browser-session snapshot
# imported by /reach-setup. Bare /reach-setup includes that sensitive Google
# snapshot only when this flag is exactly 1; it is stored mode 0600. Manual path:
# /reach-setup import_cookies vision-gemini-web. Exact-origin/network containment
# remains enforced and the isolated browser is destroyed after use. The fetch
# image/keyframe analyzers never select this route automatically.
# PI_VISION_GEMINI_WEB_ENABLED="1"
#
# Synthetic probe helpers exist for explicit diagnostics/tests, but the live
# fetch image/video hot paths do not currently probe before sending user bytes.
#
# Independent private-GitHub flag (default off; exact value required):
# Without PI_VISION_PRIVATE_GITHUB_TRANSFER=1, private/authenticated GitHub
# content never reaches any eligible cloud vision endpoint.
# PI_VISION_PRIVATE_GITHUB_TRANSFER="1"

# ── Backend control ──

# PI_SEARCH_WEB_BACKENDS="duckduckgo,brave,searxng"
# PI_SEARCH_BROWSER_ALLOW_SENSITIVE="1"
# PI_SEARCH_DESKTOP_AUTOMATION="1"

# ── User-Chrome companion bridge (optional) ──
# Single multi-companion bridge on literal 127.0.0.1:17319. Default Chrome
# onboarding is /chrome-install followed by /chrome-authorize. Install only
# prepares the local companion; authorize explicitly arms one-shot auto-find/auto-pin, and the
# first valid companion registration pins its origin (trust-on-first-use).
# Selection: auto-finds user chrome via Chromium OS-default sole match, or
# explicit /chrome-authorize <family> user choice; same-family ambiguity
# fails closed.
# PI_SEARCH_CHROME_EXTENSION_ID is OPTIONAL strict pin for the only allowed
# companion origin. Leave unset for auto-pin on first paired companion.
# PI_SEARCH_CHROME_EXTENSION_ID="abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzabcdef"
# Optional stable steady-state pairing secret. In steady state it is required
# alongside the pinned extension origin; Origin alone never authenticates a
# companion. A fresh extension cannot read host env, so explicit
# /chrome-authorize may hand this configured value to the first valid companion
# registration inside the same one-shot user-armed pairing window used by the
# generated-secret path. Other/model-triggered callers cannot open that window.
# If no companion registers during the authorize attempt, the window closes;
# rerun the command to retry. A stable value survives Pi bridge restarts once
# the browser session has learned it; a fresh browser session may need authorize
# again to receive it.
# PI_SEARCH_CHROME_PAIRING_SECRET="paste-a-long-random-code-here"

# ── Bootstrap ──

# PI_SEARCH_BOOTSTRAP="off"
# PI_SEARCH_AUTO_INSTALL="0"
# PI_SEARCH_ALLOW_INSTALL="0"
# Startup and the internal reach_setup auto action never import browser cookies.
# Bare USER slash /reach-setup is the explicit consent path: after confirmation
# it imports supported cookies and checks social backend readiness. Advanced
# /reach-setup import_cookies <provider> and login <provider> remain available.
# Kill switch for explicit cookie/session paths:
# PI_SEARCH_BROWSER_AUTOMATION="0"

# ── Output ──

# PI_SEARCH_MAX_TOOL_OUTPUT_CHARS="60000"

# ── State ──

# PI_SEARCH_STATE_DIR="$HOME/.pi-northstar"
# Opt-in Pi-session ownership for the local/development native broker. Unset/blank
# means Pi never starts broker authority. Ordinary jobs commands remain connect-only.
# Uses the same local provider-backed leaf runtime as `northstar broker serve`.
# PI_NORTHSTAR_BROKER_PROJECT_ID="local-demo"
# Optional broker runtime/state root override (default: ~/.northstar/runtime).
# NORTHSTAR_RUNTIME_DIR="$HOME/.northstar/runtime"

# ── Cookies ──

# PI_SEARCH_COOKIE_BROWSER="chrome"
# PI_SEARCH_COOKIE_STALE_MS="43200000"

# ── Cookie import via local CDP (bare /reach-setup or explicit import_cookies) ──

# BROWSER_CDP_ENDPOINT="http://127.0.0.1:9222"

# ── Cua Driver (desktop) ──


# ── Scrapling (auto-detected if installed) ──
# If scrapling Python package is installed, Pi-Northstar uses it automatically
# for JS-rendered pages and Cloudflare bypass. No config needed.
# Install: pip install "scrapling[fetchers]" && scrapling install
# Optional proxy:
# PI_SEARCH_SCRAPLING_PROXY=
# SIDECAR_DEVICE=cpu
